{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfduB38ancRtLvtoGxtFY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted a portfolio, and it lost money in validation: its Sharpe was **−0.61** (2000–2012), against +0.22 in training. It splits risk equally between two parts: an equal-weight long position in all 47 market-hedged industries (`hold_*`), and the 47 five-day time-series momentum signals (`tsmom_*_5`), scaled so both parts have the same training volatility.\n\nI built it from the training data only, then ran one validation backtest and submitted that result without tuning on validation. In training, most signals didn't hold up. Across the two halves of 1970–1999, individual signal Sharpes correlated at only 0.07. When I tested fitted weightings in training by refitting each year on past data, they did worse than simple fixed baskets (Sharpe between −0.26 and +0.04). The only two groups that were positive in 4 of 5 training sub-periods were the hold and 5-day momentum baskets, so I combined them.\n\nI could have raised the validation number a lot by searching over weights with the 5,000 backtest calls. I didn't, because a portfolio picked that way would be fitted to 2000–2012 and its validation Sharpe would no longer be a real out-of-sample result. The negative number is therefore an honest estimate.\n\nIn two sentences: the final portfolio splits risk equally between an equal-weight long in all 47 market-hedged industries and the 47 five-day momentum signals, chosen because they were the only groups that held up across training sub-periods. Built from 1970–1999 data alone, it had a training Sharpe of 0.22 but a validation Sharpe of −0.61, which suggests these industry signals have little lasting edge."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":797,"cache_read_input_tokens":10408,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_in